course: dictionary
lesson: 6538
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. Neutralize is one of them. In British English it is spelled neutralise, but the meaning is the same.
explain: In science, to neutralize means to mix an acid with a base (an alkali) so the result is neither acid nor alkali. «Toothpaste can neutralize acid in your mouth.» The science words around it tell you the meaning. Another meaning is to stop something from having an effect, so it no longer does harm: «The baking soda will neutralize the smell.»
explain: In war or crime stories, neutralize can be a formal, calm-sounding word for stopping or killing an enemy. «The target was neutralized» hides a serious event behind a quiet word. That kind of mild word for something harsh is called a euphemism. Ask what the words around it are about, and what the writer is really saying.
fact: Neutralize means to stop something from having an effect, or to mix an acid with a base so the result is neither acid nor alkali.
fact: Neutralise is the British spelling of neutralize, and both mean the same thing.
fact: In science, baking soda and indigestion tablets neutralize acid.
fact: A euphemism is a mild word used instead of a harsh one; in military reports, «neutralized» can mean killed.
fact: To work out which meaning fits, check the subject: is it science, a smell, or a war or spy story?
exercise: «Toothpaste can neutralize acid in your mouth.» What does neutralize mean here? | To make the toothpaste bigger | *To reduce the acid so it is no longer harmful | To paint the inside of your mouth | To heat the acid until it boils
exercise: «The baking soda will neutralize the smell in the fridge.» What does neutralize mean? | *To stop the smell having an effect | To spread the smell further | To make the fridge colder | To sing a song about the smell
exercise: In a soldier's report, «neutralized» most likely means | Moved to a new house | Made into a tidy list | Pleased and happy | *Stopped, or killed
test: «Indigestion tablets neutralize stomach acid.» In science, what happens to the acid? | *It is made neither acid nor alkali | It disappears into the air | It becomes twice as strong | It turns into a sweet drink
test: Which word is the British spelling of neutralize? | neutrality | *neutralise | neutralized | neutral
exam: «The team had to neutralize the threat quickly.» In this game story, what does neutralize most likely mean? | Ask the threat for a drink | *Stop the threat from causing harm | Write the threat in a diary | Make the threat bigger
